# For Friends Who Ask... — Part 2
Tamil title: நண்பர்கள் கேட்பதற்கு... — பகுதி 2

## 13. Was the prohibitory law truly born from fear that Indian unity and sovereignty would be damaged and the country fragmented? — continued

The DMK had no presence in the other Dravidian states of Andhra, Kerala and Karnataka. The ruling party used to ask: “In this condition, who will respect or accept their plan?” This must not be forgotten.

We too said that support in the other Dravidian states was insufficient, but that as it grew steadily in Tamil Nadu, support would slowly but surely arise there as well; our propaganda must be arranged accordingly, and we were trying to do so. That too must not be forgotten.

Thus there was no immediate prospect of Dravida Nadu becoming a separate country within months or even years. The ruling party knew this perfectly well. The law was therefore not introduced from fear that the country would soon be fragmented.

Although the time when the DMK could obtain Dravida Nadu was not near, Congress understood that the time when the DMK might defeat it in Tamil Nadu and become the ruling party was drawing close. Tiruchengode and Tiruvannamalai increased that fear.

Patil said that Kamaraj wanted to leave office specifically to work against the DMK, and that this was later made a plan for all India. That is the truth. The claim that the ban was needed to prevent fragmentation merely mesmerises the public and offers the world an explanation. The real fear was that the DMK would become the ruling party; the law was intended to prevent it.

Anyone examining the issue from several angles can understand this. Kamaraj resigned office with precisely this confidence. Like a crow alighting as a palmyra fruit happens to fall, his resignation and the arrival of the ban appeared to coincide. He expected the DMK to defy the law and destroy itself, leaving Congress without electoral opposition and allowing it to plant the flag of victory. He could then wear a garland of praise for his extraordinary ability.

Understanding this, the DMK refused to fall into the pit Congress had dug. That is the reason for Kamaraj's anger.

If the law truly aimed only to prevent the country's division, Kamaraj should rejoice that the DMK abandoned partition. Instead, he is furious. He had expected “those fellows” to defy the ban and disappear, but they disappointed him; now the DMK will trouble Congress at the next election. Hence his weary anger.

Does that not itself show the prohibition was introduced not through fear of national division, but to keep the DMK from the electoral field? Had preserving unity been the true object, such a law would have appeared when the Nagaland issue grew.

The ban is certainly the story of pounding the mortar while thinking of beaten rice—its declared object and real target are different.

## 14. You say the law was intended to prevent the DMK contesting elections. Let us accept that. Must the DMK participate in elections?

Congress rule conducts itself autocratically.

Without a strong opposition, it has gained confidence that no one will question it however it behaves.

It has created conditions in which most periodicals can prosper only by supporting and publicising Congress.

Using taxpayers' money to establish radio, cinema and similar media, it employs them for party propaganda.

Under the name of information and publicity, it spends enormous sums advertising the Congress party.

It turns public-welfare schemes created for society generally into instruments of party publicity.

Loans, grants, ploughs, bullocks, fertiliser and other facilities meant to assist cultivators are administered so Congress members can obtain them most easily.

Even permission to establish industries is granted through a partisan lens.

It has turned even the pure institution of cooperation into pieces upon the party chessboard.

Under the pretext of industrial development, it has strengthened capitalism.

Even after laws supposedly abolished landlordism, it grants exemptions enabling Congress favourites to remain lords.

Not content with capitalists and landlords, it uses licence and permit systems in commerce to create sudden millionaires who feed and cultivate the party.

It sees great capitalists evade income tax on an enormous scale, pretends not to notice and becomes an accomplice to their fraud.

Though aware of black markets and predatory profit, it does not abolish them but uses them for party advantage.

It leaves bribery and corruption penetrating administration from the lowest to the highest levels.

It burdens administration with useless departments and excess personnel, making it sluggish, disordered and extravagantly expensive.

It spends vast sums upon plans without securing proportional benefit and thereby wastes public money.

Even after pouring out crores, it has failed to reach adequate food production.

It cruelly imposes indirect taxes affecting poor and middle-class people on a scale far greater than direct taxes upon the wealthy.

When cultivators seek rights, workers ask higher wages or ordinary employees request remedies, it pounces upon and suppresses them while supporting capitalists.

It refuses bold action against rising prices that torment poor and middle-class people.

It lacks the will to restrain intermediaries who riot through industry and commerce.

By giving foreign capitalists room to grow here, it creates a grave future danger.

It misuses foreign loans so the ability to repay does not grow, creating fear that debt will become a great burden upon future generations.

It leaves banks operating chiefly as supports for capitalists, causing great national loss.

It admits that although development plans increased wealth, that wealth became trapped among a few and income did not spread enough to improve public living standards. Yet it hides its own responsibility and refuses to state and implement a remedy.

It spends enormous sums in elections and turns democracy into farce. By obtaining this money from exploiters such as Mundhra and Sirajuddin, it changes from tiger to fox.

It declares socialism its programme, yet refuses its accepted meaning and offers ever-new interpretations, making even that new ideal a mockery.

For party growth, it allies with people incapable of accepting socialism and thereby ruins democracy.

Within the Congress party responsible for government, the tradition of sacrifice has greatly diminished. People without principles and selfish exploiters have become Congressmen, captured the party and been allowed to turn administration into their puppet.

It has seized rights, unleashed repression and created public fear.

It seeks to establish Hindi imperialism and degrade speakers of other languages.

Therefore opposing Congress rule is a democrat's duty.

To perform that duty effectively, does any party in Tamil Nadu today equal the DMK in scale, organisation, propaganda machinery and legislative presence? The country admits there is none.

In that condition, what can we call those who tell us to defy the law and destroy the Kazhagam except Congress overseers? Examine it without anger.

The DMK stands forward to question and restrain fifteen years of Congress autocracy.

The DMK worked saying it would obtain the country through the ballot method. The prohibitory law sabotaged that method by saying advocates of separation could not contest elections.

When the law no longer allows the ballot to seek victory for partition, what can be done?

Those asking why we do not defy the ban are supposedly great heroes who say, “The ballot is finished; let us adopt the bullet.”

The DMK's principle is that only the ballot, never the bullet, is permissible. It has decided to use the ballot wherever and to whatever extent it remains useful. What is wrong in that?

Congress lacked courage to defeat DMK separatist propaganda through elections and therefore used the law to remove it from the ballot. Congress fears meeting the DMK in an election, and cowardice produced the ban. Since the DMK neither desires nor regards violence as proper, it can only use the ballot to the extent still available.

Is it not disgraceful to impose a ban merely because Congress cannot answer separatist speech in the electoral field? What disgrace lies in amending the party constitution to say the DMK will henceforth labour for what can be obtained through the ballot? Think about it.

## 15. They say that after abandoning partition, the DMK has no other ideal.

That is the speech of abusers, not thinkers.

The DMK possessed two ideals: obtaining liberation for the country and establishing good government.

The new law is aimed at destroying the DMK; it prohibits work towards the liberation ideal. But it cannot stop the DMK labouring to establish good government. The organisation must continue its work to secure such government.

Under good government, rights will not be taken away and partiality will not exist.

Too much authority now rests with the Central Government in Delhi; states such as Tamil Nadu possess insufficient power.

All taxes yielding rich revenue go to Delhi, while dwindling taxes and the condition of standing with an outstretched hand remain for Dravidian states.

Delhi plans the establishment of industries; state governments must plead, wait and yearn.

Delhi imposes linguistic imperialism through Hindi; Dravidian state governments must endure the destruction of their mother tongues.

The Dravida Nadu plan arose from these causes. Tamil Nadu, Andhra, Kerala and Karnataka should not hand every power to the Centre and remain like municipal councils. They should obtain full governing authority. Since all four are Dravidian states by ethnicity, language and culture, they may form a federation among themselves if they wish. That was the plan.

Even when opponents called it a scheme to cut the country apart, we explained that we were not going to detach Dravidian territory, carry it away and turn it into an island.

The amended Constitution now requires that India's union and sovereignty not be harmed. The law is justified by arguing that a separate Dravida Nadu would injure them and embolden foreign countries to attack a fragmented India.

Even when such doubts were expressed, we said an independent Dravidian country would not become India's enemy and would support India if another power attacked it.

When China invaded, the DMK felt an attack upon India as an attack upon itself and voluntarily came forward to cooperate.

Despite all this, the Indian Government considers the Dravida Nadu plan harmful to India's unity, structure and sovereignty. It amended the Constitution and removed the right to advocate such separate countries.

The law called the ban is the Sixteenth Amendment, which prohibits speech opposed to India's unity and sovereignty. Under it, not only the DMK but no party, association, forum or movement can operate without accepting the amendment. We therefore revise our party constitution, and after revision cannot advocate Dravidian separation from India as an independent country.

But the Sixteenth Amendment does not prevent us from:

- arguing and working to protect and develop Dravidian languages, ethnicity and culture;
- demanding every kind of additional power for the linguistic states Tamil Nadu, Andhra, Kerala and Karnataka;
- seeking all powers except those essential to the Indian Union and its sovereignty;
- arguing that rich-yielding taxes should belong to states rather than Delhi;
- opposing Delhi's control over industrial establishment where it produces partiality and imbalance, and seeking those powers for state governments or a Dravidian federation;
- protecting and cultivating Dravidian language, ethnicity and culture;
- gaining greater powers for the Dravidian states; and
- enabling those states, if they wish, to create a federation among themselves.

The law obstructs only the demand that such a Dravidian federation detach itself from the Indian Union.

Once this explanation is considered, comrades distressed that we abandoned the entire ideal should understand why the party constitution is being amended. We are not losing every programme and task undertaken for Dravidian life and growth. The ban prevents only separation from the Indian Union in the manner opponents described as cutting up the country.

The Kazhagam can remain organised, perform its other work and labour on every other front for Dravidian life and progress without undertaking “severance.” That is why its constitution is revised.

## 16. We obtained votes saying Dravida Nadu should be independent. Now that the plan is gone, may we remain in the Assembly? They tell us to resign.

Those telling us to resign are not the friends who helped win those Assembly seats. They joined Congress and worked intensely to keep us out, failed, and became disappointed and resentful. Can speech born of resentment seek our good?

Moreover, some used the Dravida Nadu plan to enter Parliament as DMK representatives, then left the DMK midway and said the plan was wrong, but did not relinquish their parliamentary seats. Such people now lecture us about political integrity.

Leave that aside and consider this:

When we said public support in elections showed support for an independent Dravida Nadu—

